1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Over six hundred years before the arrival of Europeans,
a great journey across the vast Pacific Ocean brought our ancestors to these islands.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,000
They came in great waaka—double-hulled canoes—
navigating by the stars, the ocean swells, and the knowledge passed down through generations.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,500
These were the Polynesian voyagers. And when they arrived at
what they called Aotearoa—the Land of the Long White Cloud—

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,000
they found a land untouched by human hands. Vast forests.
Abundant wildlife. Rivers teeming with kai moana, seafood that sustained entire iwi, entire tribes.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
They settled. They explored. They learned this new land's rhythms and seasons.
And over centuries, they built a civilization uniquely their own.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,500
By 1600, Aotearoa belonged entirely to Māori.

7
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,000
We had developed complex societies. Iwi governed their own territories—
Ngāpuhi in the north, Ngāti Tūwharetoa in the central plateau, Ngāi Tahu in the south.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Each iwi had its own identity, its own stories, its own connection to the land.
Our whānau—our families—were the center of everything.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Whānau meant not just relatives by blood, but a kinship that bound us to each other
and to the whenua, the land itself.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
We were tangata whenua. People of the land. And we knew no other way of living than this:
in harmony with Aotearoa, speaking only te reo Māori, our language—

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
the language of our tīpuna, our ancestors,
stretching back through countless generations.

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Our survival depended on understanding the land and the sea in ways
that went deeper than mere survival. It was a sacred relationship.

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
In spring, we cultivated our gardens. The kūmara—the sweet potato brought from Hawaiki
by our ancestors—grew in carefully tended soil.

14
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
We also grew taro, another precious kai whenua, food from the land.
We understood rotation, soil quality, when to plant and when to harvest.

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
This was mahi—our work—but it was also knowledge, passed from tūpuna to tamariki,
from grandparents to children.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
When hunting season came, our men tracked moa, the great flightless birds,
and deer through dense forests.

17
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
They laid snares, used spears with precision honed over generations.
Every part of the animal was used—nothing wasted.

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,000
The bones became tools. The hides became clothing.
Nothing was taken without gratitude.

19
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
But our most abundant food came from the moana, the ocean.
We fished with nets woven from native flax.

20
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
We dove for kina and pāua. We gathered pipi and tuatua from the shores.
The kai moana sustained us.

21
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
It was our supermarket, our pharmacy, our security.
And we built homes worthy of our mahi.

22
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Our whare were constructed with precision and care.
Wooden frames lashed together with materials from the land.

23
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Thatch roofs that shed rain and kept warmth inside. Inside the whare,
a central pūtātā—a fire—where whānau gathered to cook, to talk, to share stories.

24
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Some of us built pā—fortified villages positioned on hilltops
overlooking valleys and waterways.

25
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,000
These were not just defensive structures. They were centers of community.
From the pā, we could see our crops. We could watch for danger. We could gather together.

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Every season had its own rhythm. Every mahi had its purpose.
We lived not just on the land—we lived with the land,

27
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
understanding its patterns, its gifts, its requirements.
There was no separation between us and Aotearoa.

28
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
We were of this place. It was of us.
To be Māori was to carry your whakapapa—your genealogy—in your heart and on your tongue.

29
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Your whakapapa connected you to your iwi, to your ancestors, to the gods themselves.
To recite one's whakapapa was to claim identity.

30
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
It was to say: "I know who I am. I know where I come from."
The tohunga—our experts and priests—were the keepers of this knowledge.

31
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
A tohunga whakairo was a master carver. A tohunga tapua was a spiritual expert.
A tohunga kōrero was a keeper of stories.

32
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Each had spent years, sometimes decades, mastering their craft and knowledge.
Knowledge in our culture was sacred.

33
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
It was not written in books—it was carried in memory, in practice,
in the careful teaching of the next generation.

34
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
This is why the kōrero—the talking, the discussion—was so important.
When important decisions needed to be made, we gathered for hui.

35
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
We sat together. We listened. We spoke. We debated.
We reached consensus through the power of collective wisdom.

36
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
And we expressed our identity through the arts.
Our tohunga whakairo carved intricate patterns into wood and bone.

37
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Each pattern had meaning. Each spiral and line told a story or represented a genealogy.
The whakairo wasn't decoration—it was history frozen in wood.

38
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Our wāhine—our women—performed the ancient art of raranga, weaving.
With flax from our land, they created baskets, mats, and clothing of stunning beauty and durability.

39
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Patterns were handed down through generations. Each design carried meaning.
And we gathered to perform the haka—the traditional dance that expressed our power, our unity,

40
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
our fierce pride as Māori. The haka was not entertainment.
It was ceremony. It was the expression of our collective wairua—our spirit.

41
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
We also sang waiata—our songs—which carried our stories.
Through waiata, we remembered great victories. We mourned our dead.

42
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
We celebrated love and whānau. The waiata kept our history alive.
Our spiritual beliefs ran deep. We believed in atua—gods—who inhabited every part of nature.

43
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Tangaroa ruled the moana. Tāne guarded the forests.
Papatūānuku was the earth mother.

44
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Every mountain, every river, every tree had its own mauri—its own spiritual essence.
We performed karakia—prayers and incantations—to acknowledge these forces and seek their protection.

45
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Before we hunted, we performed karakia. Before we planted, karakia.
These were not superstitions—they were expressions of respect and gratitude

46
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
for the life we took and the gifts we received.
This was our culture. This was our way.

47
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
But at the heart of all of this—at the very center of Māori identity—was te reo Māori.
Our language. Te reo Māori was not just a way to communicate.

48
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
It was a way of seeing the world. In English, we might say "the ocean."
In te reo Māori, we say "te moana"—and that phrase carries within it

49
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,000
centuries of relationship, of respect, of knowledge about the ocean.
When we said "tangata whenua"—people of the land—

50
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
those three words held an entire philosophy. We were not separate from the land.
We belonged to it. We were part of it.

51
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
When we spoke of our "tīpuna"—our ancestors—we invoked their presence.
They were not distant history. They were with us, guiding us, speaking through us.

52
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Our language encoded our values. "Manaakitanga" means hospitality and care for others—
but it goes deeper. It means recognizing the mauri, the essential life force, in every person.

53
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
"Kaitiakitanga" means guardianship of the land—but it's not ownership.
It's a sacred responsibility to protect and nurture what has been entrusted to us.

54
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
"Whanaungatanga" speaks to relationships and kinship—but it extends beyond family.
It's about the connections that bind us all together.

55
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Every word in te reo Māori carried this depth. When you spoke our language,
you were speaking the worldview of your ancestors.

56
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
You were participating in thousands of years of Polynesian wisdom.
Before 1600, every Māori child learned te reo Māori as their first and only language.

57
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
It was the water we swam in. The air we breathed.
The voice of our tīpuna speaking through us.

58
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
We recited karakia in te reo Māori. We sang waiata in te reo Māori.
We debated in hui using te reo Māori.

59
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
We told our stories, our histories, our genealogies in te reo Māori.
In 1600, Aotearoa belonged to us.

60
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Our iwi governed these lands. Our whānau thrived. Our culture flourished.
Our language filled the air from the northern tip of the North Island

61
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
to the southern reaches of the South Island. We were free. We were whole.
We were Māori. Te reo Māori was the voice of that world.

62
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
It was the expression of our identity, our values, our connection to each other
and to this sacred land.

63
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
This is the Aotearoa that existed before colonisation.
This is the world our ancestors knew. This is the language they spoke.

64
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
This is the legacy we carry within us today. Because even though te reo Māori was nearly lost,
it was never truly silenced.

65
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
And today, as we revive our language, we reclaim our identity.
We honor our ancestors. We speak as our tīpuna spoke.

66
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Kia ora. Thank you for listening to our story.
